
Archaeologist

Specializing in the study of lost civilizations, ancient
ruins, and uncharted lands, Archaeologists bring the
light of discovery to the dark and deadly places of the
world. Doing their best to uncover the wisdom of ages
past, they tend to feel kinship with the civilizations they
study and go to great lengths not to offend their memory.
Student of Archaeology

3rd-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
You gain proficiency in both History and Investigation.
If you are already proficient in History or Investigation,
you instead add your Intellect Die to any ability check you make with that skill.

Time spent exploring ancient places has given you a knack for navigating their dangers, granting you the following skills:

    You learn to speak, read, and write two extra languages
    of your choice, often archaic or forgotten tongues.
    Any time you make an ability check related to a trap, you add your Intellect Die to the result of your roll.
    If you spend at least 1 minute examining an object, you can ascertain its value, origin, and age. If it has magical properties, you learn them as if you had cast identify.

Eye for Antiquity

3rd-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
Your knowledge of ancient civilizations allows you to unlock lost secrets of their technology. When you spend a long rest in a Settlement or dungeon, you can spend 1 hour examining antiques and debris to unearth one Curio of your choice.

The Curio is a Tiny magical item with the properties of one Common magical item of your choice. If the Curio requires a Spellcasting Ability, you use your Intelligence for it.

Due to advanced age and fragility, you must spend 1 hour during each long rest maintaining your Curios, or they revert into mundane objects. You can maintain a number of Curios equal to your Intelligence modifier (a minimum of one).
Adventuring Academic

7th-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
Your ability to navigate deadly ruins in search of knowledge is without peer. Whenever you make a saving throw to avoid the effects of a trap, you add your Intellect Die to your roll.

You also gain a climb speed equal to your walking speed

??? info "Ancient Artifacts"

    Eye for Antiquity is a rare ability that gives access to magical items of your choice. Discuss with your DM to determine items appropriate to your game.


Ancient Insights

7th-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
You have advanced in your understanding of ancient
technologies. You ignore all class, race, and alignment restrictions for the attunement and use of magic items, scrolls, and potions.

If such a magical item, scroll, or potion produces a spell
that requires concentration, you can maintain concentration on the spell and your Focus at the same time. However, if you are forced to make a saving throw to maintain concentration, you make a single saving throw for both effects.
Lore Master

13th-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
You study every detail of every myth, legend, and folk tale you come across. When you observe a person, place, or object for at least 10 minutes, you mystically recall information about it as if it were the target of a legend lore spell.

The target of this feature does not need to be of legendary importance in order for you to gain information. However, if there is no relevant lore about the target, you learn nothing.
Unearthed Arcana

13th-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
You are an unparalleled expert with ancient magical devices. When you use Eye for Antiquity, you can unearth Curios with the properties of Common or Uncommon magic items.

You can also use your Intellect save DC for your magic item saving throws unless the magic item's innate DC is higher.
Master Archaeologist

18th-level Archaeologist Discipline feature
Exposure to the magic of the ancient world has given you an innate resilience to its effects. You gain Resistance to damage from spells, magic items, and magical traps.

Finally, you can have a single Curio with the properties of a Rare magic item.

